  "worked_solution": "To find the interquartile range, we first need to find the lower quartile and the upper quartile.\n\nThe lower quartile of a random variable $X$ is the number ${q}_{1}$ such that\n\n$$\nP(X\\le {q}_{1})=\\frac{1}{4},\n$$\n\nand the upper quartile is the number ${q}_{3}$ such that\n\n$$\nP(X\\le {q}_{3})=\\frac{3}{4}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the lower quartile:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le {q}_{1}) & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ F({q}_{1}) & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ 1-{e}^{1-{q}_{1}} & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ {e}^{1-{q}_{1}} & =\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ 1-{q}_{1} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{3}{4}) \\\\ {q}_{1} & =1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{3}{4})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we find the upper quartile:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le {q}_{3}) & =\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ F({q}_{3}) & =\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ 1-{e}^{1-{q}_{3}} & =\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ {e}^{1-{q}_{3}} & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ 1-{q}_{3} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{4}) \\\\ {q}_{3} & =1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{4})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the interquartile range is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}IQR & ={q}_{3}-{q}_{1} \\\\  & =1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{4})-(1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{3}{4})) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{3}{4})-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{4}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3).\\end{aligned}\n$$",
